Subject: [PATCH 24/24] the Forth/FCode trampoline ran in the CLIENT's segments
Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

TODO 13.3(A) recorded x86's client context reading different values than the
prompt, and said in as many words that no single-mechanism story explained it:

    word         xt @prompt   xt @context   value @prompt   value @context
    load-base    13756c       12ebb8        e066fdd0        4000000
    load-size    12afc8       12afc8        1f              0

    "Two mechanisms, one symptom ... a LOOKUP difference ... a FETCH
     difference.  Not a lookup problem at all."

There is one mechanism. Both rows are the same firmware being read through the
wrong window.

WHAT arch/x86/context.c DID. arch_init_program() fills the client context with
"reasonable default for flat memory model" -- FLAT_CS/FLAT_DS, whose GDT base is
0 -- and then switches to those for everything except file-type 1, which gets
RELOC_CS/RELOC_DS (base = virt_offset). That is right for a client program. It
is wrong for the two file types whose ENTRY IS FIRMWARE CODE:

    libopenbios/forth_load.c   PUSH((ucell)&init_forth_context)
    libopenbios/fcode_load.c   PUSH((ucell)&init_fcode_context)

Those trampolines are firmware functions that go on to drive the Forth
interpreter, and x86 relocates OpenBIOS by REBASING THE GDT (arch/x86/segment.c),
so every Forth address is segment-relative. Entered flat, the trampoline reads
each address at its link-time value -- where the ORIGINAL, un-relocated copy of
the image still sits, byte-exact, frozen at the instant of relocation. Nothing
faults. The copy is there and is valid. It is merely STALE.

MEASURED 2026-08-26, x86, `load /ide@1/cdrom@0:\go.fth` then `go`. virt_offset
recovered from the running firmware as `400000 load-base -` = 1fd8fe50, so the
stale window is read from the prompt as `<addr> 400000 load-base - -`:

    forth-wordlist @     live 137808     stale 132b08
    file-size @          live 16         stale 0

That is the whole of both rows:

  * the stale chain head (132b08) predates the `constant load-base` that
    arch/x86/openbios.c defines at the end of arch_init (137808), so $find walks
    straight past the shadow to nvram.fs's config word. The SAME search, over an
    older chain. Not a different search order: #order, context[0] and current
    are identical in both windows -- checked.
  * `load-size` is `file-size @` and its xt is genuinely the same word. The
    address it fetches is the same too. The MEMORY at that address is not.

And it is why amd64 passed throughout: amd64 does not relocate, virt_offset is
0, and flat IS reloc. The arch that could not show the bug is the one that
looked healthy -- which is the fourth time in this lab that the passing arch was
the one running less.

THE FIX, in two parts.

(1) arch/x86/context.c enters the fcode/forth trampolines in the firmware's own
    relocated segments, as it already did for a type-1 client, and returns
    before laying down the five client-entry arguments -- a C function with no
    parameters wants none. The two type numbers are read from the Forth
    constants (`fcode`, `forth`) rather than written here as 10 and 11: a number
    copied into C is a cache of a fact that lives in
    forth/debugging/client.fs:76-86.

(2) libopenbios/{forth,fcode}_load.c stamp ls.file-type in *_init_program(),
    not only in *_load(). The type is a property of the entry installed one line
    above, but only the C loader path wrote it -- `load <path>` goes through
    $load, which calls the DEVICE PACKAGE's own `load` method and never touches
    the field, so on that path it kept the 0 that client.fs erases it to, and 0
    means elf-boot. Before this, a Forth payload loaded with `load` reached
    arch_init_program() claiming to be an elf-boot image and was handed
    elf-boot's segments and elf-boot's 0xe1fb007 magic in eax. Same shape as the
    two-records-of-one-fact defect patch 23 fixed one field over.

WATCHED TO BITE. `./smoke-openbios.sh client-forth` is the new track: x86 and
amd64 each `load` a Forth payload off a CD and `go` it, and must print the
payload's own marker. Re-injecting (1) -- flat segments for the trampolines
again -- fails it by name on x86 while amd64 still passes, which is exactly the
asymmetry above. The obvious re-injection does not even compile
(-Werror=unused-but-set-variable on t_fcode/t_forth), so the control uses an
unsatisfiable condition instead.

The track also proves x86's two windows still DIFFER before its pass counts --
on an arch where they are the same memory, running the trampoline in the wrong
one is undetectable -- and carries a same-probe tautology (a cell compared with
itself must report -1) so that a `=` answering 0 for everything cannot satisfy
it. The negative control is a second boot with the same program minus is_forth()'s
`\ ` magic: it must be refused by name, not evaluated.

diff --git a/arch/x86/context.c b/arch/x86/context.c
--- a/arch/x86/context.c
+++ b/arch/x86/context.c
@@ -110,6 +110,15 @@
 {
     struct context volatile *ctx = __context;
     ucell type, entry, param;
+    ucell t_fcode, t_forth;
+
+    /* The two file types whose ENTRY IS FIRMWARE CODE, read from the Forth
+     * constants rather than written here as 10 and 11
+     * (forth/debugging/client.fs:76-86): a number copied into C is a cache of
+     * a fact that lives somewhere else. */
+    feval("fcode"); t_fcode = POP();
+    feval("forth"); t_forth = POP();
+
     /* Fill in reasonable default for flat memory model */
     ctx->gdt_base = virt_to_phys(gdt);
     ctx->gdt_limit = GDT_LIMIT;
@@ -147,7 +156,7 @@
      * the client stack where its _start(residual, entry, cif, args, argslen)
      * reads them. param[0..4] sit just above return_addr per ESP_LOC, so on
      * entry the client sees them as its C arguments 1..5. */
-    if (type == 1) {
+    if (type == 1 || type == t_fcode || type == t_forth) {
         /* Enter the client with the firmware's RELOCATED segments, not the
          * flat ones an elf-boot image gets. x86 relocates OpenBIOS by rebasing
          * the GDT (arch/x86/segment.c), so firmware pointers are
@@ -165,7 +174,35 @@
          * window below _start (0x100010), which maps to physical memory just
          * below the relocated firmware. That window tracks RAM size
          * automatically, because both ends move with virt_offset.
-         */
+         *
+         * THE fcode/forth TYPES ARE NOT CLIENT PROGRAMS AT ALL, and they are
+         * here for a stronger version of the same reason. Their `entry` is the
+         * address of a FIRMWARE FUNCTION -- init_fcode_context() /
+         * init_forth_context() in libopenbios/initprogram.c -- which then
+         * drives the Forth interpreter. Run flat, that trampoline reads every
+         * Forth address at its LINK-TIME value, which on this arch still holds
+         * the original, un-relocated copy of the firmware image: a byte-exact
+         * snapshot frozen at the instant of relocation. Nothing faults, because
+         * that copy is there and is valid; it is simply STALE.
+         *
+         * Measured on x86, 2026-08-26, `load` of a 0x16-byte Forth file then
+         * `go` (virt_offset = 0x1fd8fe50, so linear = forth address + that):
+         *
+         *   forth-wordlist @   live 0x137808   stale 0x132b08
+         *   file-size @        live 0x16       stale 0
+         *
+         * Those are the two symptoms TODO 13.3(A) recorded as a "lookup
+         * difference" and a "fetch difference" and could not fit into one
+         * story. They are one story: the SAME search over an older chain (the
+         * stale head predates the `constant load-base` arch_init defines, so
+         * $find walks past it to nvram.fs's config word), and the SAME address
+         * fetched in the other window. The trampoline was reading a firmware
+         * that had stopped existing before Forth ran a single word.
+         *
+         * amd64 is unaffected and always was: it does not relocate, so
+         * virt_offset is 0 and the two windows are the same memory. That is
+         * why the arch this bug hides on is the one that passes.
+         *         */
         ctx->cs = RELOC_CS;
         ctx->ds = RELOC_DS;
         ctx->es = RELOC_DS;
@@ -178,6 +215,11 @@
         ctx->esp = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)ESP_LOC(ctx);
         ctx->return_addr = (uint32_t)(uintptr_t)__exit_context;
 
+        /* Client-entry arguments: for a real client program only. The
+         * trampolines are C functions that take none. */
+        if (type != 1)
+            return 0;
+
         ctx->param[0] = 0;                                   /* residual data */
         ctx->param[1] = entry;                               /* program entry */
         ctx->param[2] = (ucell)(uintptr_t)of_client_interface; /* the CIF */
diff --git a/libopenbios/forth_load.c b/libopenbios/forth_load.c
--- a/libopenbios/forth_load.c
+++ b/libopenbios/forth_load.c
@@ -82,6 +82,24 @@
     PUSH((ucell)&init_forth_context);
     feval("load-state >ls.entry !");
 
+    /* LOCAL DIVERGENCE (mklab). STAMP THE TYPE HERE, NOT ONLY IN forth_load().
+     *
+     * The file type is a property of the ENTRY installed on the line above --
+     * this is the Forth trampoline, so the type IS `forth`, whoever read the
+     * bytes. But only forth_load() wrote it, and forth_load() runs on the C
+     * loader path alone. `load <path>` goes through $load
+     * (forth/debugging/client.fs), which calls the DEVICE PACKAGE's own `load`
+     * method and never touches ls.file-type -- so on that path the field kept
+     * the value client.fs erased it to, which is 0, which means `elf-boot`.
+     *
+     * Measured on x86 before this: a Forth payload loaded with `load` reached
+     * arch_init_program() claiming to be an elf-boot image, and was handed
+     * elf-boot's segments and elf-boot's 0xe1fb007 magic in eax.
+     *
+     * Same shape as the two records of one fact in initprogram.c: a field
+     * three paths maintain and a fourth does not is worse than absent. */
+    feval("forth load-state >ls.file-type !");
+
     arch_init_program();
     
     feval("-1 state-valid !");
diff --git a/libopenbios/fcode_load.c b/libopenbios/fcode_load.c
--- a/libopenbios/fcode_load.c
+++ b/libopenbios/fcode_load.c
@@ -93,6 +93,12 @@
     PUSH((ucell)&init_fcode_context);
     feval("load-state >ls.entry !");
 
+    /* Stamp the type here for the same reason forth_init_program() does: the
+     * entry installed above IS the FCode trampoline, and the $load path never
+     * sets ls.file-type, so without this it reaches arch_init_program()
+     * looking like an elf-boot image. */
+    feval("fcode load-state >ls.file-type !");
+
     arch_init_program();
     
     feval("-1 state-valid !");
